","route":"met"},{"requirement_ref":"subregion_a1:listed_impact_assessment","route":"met"},{"requirement_ref":"subregion_a1:professional_secrecy_duty","route":"met"}],"reasoning":"The transfer is within Region A's material and territorial scope because it involves identified personal, health and genetic information processed through an establishment in Subregion A1. The treatment second opinion is supported by the healthcare-processing ground and by the Article 8(2)(c) exception, because the processing is necessary for care and is carried out under registered practitioners subject to professional secrecy. The patient's specific written consent also satisfies the consent and A1 disclosure requirements. See A-REG 2#L1, A-REG 3#L1, A-REG 4#L2, A-REG 4#L3, A-REG 4#L4, A-REG 6#L1, A-REG 8#L5, A1-HIA 4#L1, A1-HIA 5#L1, A1-HIA 5#L2.

The consent-only outward-transfer route cannot be used: Article 19(1)(a) is unavailable for this genetic-information transfer under A1-HIA 7#L1. Recognition is also unavailable because the recipient lacks certification and the information is sensitive. See A-REG 16#L1, A-REG 17#L3, A-REG Annex II#L1, A1-HIA 7#L1. The transfer can proceed after the parties execute Commission-approved transfer clauses, Kestrel performs the required assessment of Region B law and practice—including public-authority access powers—and adopts supplementary measures where necessary. See A-REG 18#L1, A-REG 18#L2, A-REG 18#L3.

Region B's Health Records Act operational provisions do not apply to Harrow as described: Harrow is not a covered provider or service agent, and it is not a data host because it retains the information for its own care rather than on behalf of another person. See B-HRA 2#L3, B-HRA 2#L4, B-HRA 2#L5, B-HRA 3#L1, B-HRA 3#L2, B-HRA 3#L3. The possible Records Access Act production-order power does not establish a present obstacle; it applies to a computing provider and only for national security or serious-crime investigations. See B-RAA 2#L3, B-RAA 3#L1, B-RAA 3#L2.","quote_ids":["A-REG 2#L1","A-REG 3#L1","A-REG 4#L2","A-REG 4#L3","A-REG 4#L4","A-REG 6#L1","A-REG 8#L5","A-REG 16#L1","A-REG 17#L3","A-REG 18#L1","A-REG 18#L2","A-REG 18#L3","A-REG Annex II#L1","A1-HIA 4#L1","A1-HIA 5#L1","A1-HIA 5#L2","A1-HIA 7#L1","B-HRA 2#L3","B-HRA 2#L4","B-HRA 2#L5","B-HRA 3#L1","B-HRA 3#L2","B-HRA 3#L3","B-RAA 2#L3","B-RAA 3#L1","B-RAA 3#L2"],"conflicts":[],"summary":"The transfer is not lawful as currently described because it relies on consent alone for an outward transfer containing genetic information. It is satisfiable with changes through approved transfer clauses, completion of the Article 18 third-territory assessment, and any necessary supplementary measures, together with completion of the outstanding Region A accountability, information and security documentation.","blockers":[],"notes":["Do not proceed on the consent-only outward-transfer route; A1-HIA 7 expressly rules out Article 19(1)(a) for genetic information.","The supplied consent remains effective for the underlying treatment processing and professional-secrecy disclosure; it does not substitute for an Article 18 transfer mechanism.","The encrypted upload is supportive evidence of transmission security, but the custodian must ensure that the broader Article 5 and Article 14 measures are appropriate to risk and demonstrable. See A-REG 5#L2, A-REG 5#L7, A-REG 5#L8, A-REG 14#L1.","Before proceeding, complete the Article 18 assessment, execute the approved clauses, and adopt supplementary measures if required. The portal operator's status and any applicable public-authority access powers should be addressed in that assessment.","Maintain the written processing record required by Article 13 and provide any missing Article 11 information, including the transfer route relied on. See A-REG 11#L1, A-REG 11#L2, A-REG 13#L1.","The one-person, own-record clinical referral does not trigger the stated Region A or A1 impact-assessment lists on the facts supplied. See A-REG 12#L2, A-REG 12#L5, A1-HIA 8#L1, A1-HIA 8#L2, A1-HIA 8#L3."]}
